This file answers ONE question: does consuming zig-libs oblige you to anything
beyond MIT? §1 answers it, and §0 says what has to be entered here to get
there. It is NOT a catalogue of the collection's provenance — design
references, and the licence arguments behind them, are recorded at the module
that has them (§2). A module's internal development origin — which of the
authors' own projects it was first prototyped in — is not recorded anywhere: it
carries no attribution weight and would read backwards once those projects
import zig-libs.

====================================================================
0. POLICY — what requires an entry here
====================================================================

A public specification or RFC is not itself a copyrightable work (merger
doctrine: a protocol/format it describes has essentially one correct
expression, and facts/ideas are not protected) — implementing one, however
closely, is not "derived from" anyone's code. Accordingly:

  - A module that is pure clean-room-from-a-public-spec/RFC, with NO
    third-party source code ported and NO third-party implementation studied as
    a design reference, needs NO entry in this file. Its RFC/spec citation
    lives in the module's own SPEC.md instead — see e.g. whois (RFC 3912),
    rdap (RFC 7480-family), tar (POSIX ustar + documented GNU extensions).
  - Third-party source actually ported/translated, or third-party DATA
    reproduced, requires its license terms reproduced — in
    `modules/<name>/NOTICE`, beside the files, so the notice travels with what
    owes it. Never in this file; this file only lists which modules those are.
  - A DESIGN REFERENCE — a named third-party implementation consulted for
    behavior, algorithm shape, or API design, with no source copied — is
    recorded for provenance so any future line-level reuse is caught, WITH ITS
    UPSTREAM LICENSE NAME. These are records, not conditions, and they live at
    the module (§2), never here: a repository-level file that also catalogues
    84 modules' design references stops being readable as the answer to "what
    do I owe?", which is the only question it exists to answer.
  - Exercising an installed third-party binary purely as a black-box
    compatibility test oracle (e.g. running `tar`/`nft` and diffing output
    against this module's own encoder/decoder) is neither of the above and
    needs no entry — no source or design was consulted, only the program's
    observable behavior against its own documented format. This covers
    CAPTURING that output once and committing it as a golden fixture, not only
    invoking the tool live: `tar`'s `write_golden_*.tar` are GNU tar's bytes
    wrapped around a `hello.txt` we wrote, and freezing them so the test needs
    no subprocess changes nothing about who authored what. The clause was
    written for live invocation, and this sentence exists because two
    independent readings in the 2026-08-12 audit found it did not literally
    reach the frozen case.

    THE LINE THIS DOES NOT CROSS. The exemption holds when the captured
    artifact is OUR content in the tool's format. It stops the moment the
    artifact also carries content the tool itself authored — a stock template,
    a default stylesheet, generated boilerplate — since that is no longer
    merely "the program's observable behavior" on our input. `zipstream`'s
    `xlsx_sample.xlsx` is exactly that case and is why the module carries its
    own provenance note: most of its bytes are LibreOffice's default theme and
    style tables, not ours. It equally stops at data that was never observed
    from a running program at all — numbers read out of an upstream source file
    by a script are reproduced data, not oracle output; `rescue` is that case.
    When in doubt, open the artifact; a fixture's description of itself is a
    claim, not a verification.

====================================================================
1. REQUIRED ATTRIBUTION — ported code
====================================================================

NONE at the repository level. zig-libs imposes no redistribution condition
beyond its own MIT license.

TWENTY-FOUR modules port third-party code or reproduce third-party data whose
notice must be preserved. Each carries its own `NOTICE` beside its source —
first line `<name> — third-party attribution` — stating what was taken and
reproducing that upstream's terms in full. These are the ONLY files in the
repository carrying a condition that names anyone other than the zig-libs
authors, and every one of those conditions is the same: keep the notice with
the files. To answer "does consuming zig-libs oblige me to anything beyond
MIT?", read these and nothing else:

  `modules/bech32/NOTICE`, `modules/bitcoinscript/NOTICE`,
  `modules/bitcointx/NOTICE`, `modules/brotli/NOTICE`,
  `modules/csvstream/NOTICE`, `modules/decimal/NOTICE`,
  `modules/encoding/NOTICE`, `modules/falcon/NOTICE`,
  `modules/iec62351/NOTICE`, `modules/imap/NOTICE`, `modules/json5/NOTICE`,
  `modules/l2disco/NOTICE`, `modules/lninvoice/NOTICE`,
  `modules/lnwire/NOTICE`, `modules/metrics/NOTICE`,
  `modules/minisign/NOTICE`, `modules/openapi/NOTICE`,
  `modules/poseidon/NOTICE`, `modules/psbt/NOTICE`, `modules/rescue/NOTICE`,
  `modules/security-headers/NOTICE`, `modules/tracecontext/NOTICE`,
  `modules/validate/NOTICE`, `modules/xml/NOTICE`

A new module in that position follows the same pattern: a
`modules/<name>/NOTICE` beside the files that owe it, and its path added above.

A SECOND KIND of `modules/<name>/NOTICE` exists and is deliberately not in that
list: a **provenance note**, first line `<name> — provenance note`, which
records design references and argues why nothing is owed. Those impose NO
condition. `zig build check-catalog` enforces both that the two kinds stay
distinguishable by that first line and that only the condition-bearing ones are
listed here; CONVENTIONS.md §5 decides which kind a module needs, not size.

====================================================================
2. DESIGN REFERENCES — recorded at the module, not here
====================================================================

Design references live at the module that has them: in
`modules/<name>/README.md`'s `Provenance:` statement — the default, and the
only provenance location `zig build check-catalog` reads — or, when the record
needs more than a paragraph or makes a licensing argument that must not be
compressed, in a `modules/<name>/NOTICE` provenance note. They moved out of
this file on 2026-08-14, where they had run to ~1000 lines indexing 84 modules.
`modules/<name>/SPEC.md` is deliberately NOT a destination: it is the
verification-methodology file, and it is where "studied as a design reference"
most easily drifts into "verified against".

None of those records imposes any obligation beyond zig-libs' own MIT license,
even though the projects they name include GPL-2.0, GPL-3.0, LGPL-2.1,
LGPL-3.0 and AGPL-3.0 ones — a design reference carries no license condition at
all. A module with no such record needs none: pure clean-room from a public
spec/RFC, per §0. A full provenance/license audit was completed 2026-07-09 and
re-examined 2026-07-29; both are recorded in the project git history.
